{"comments":[{"id":"03d4ef8b-cd26-47d7-b2fe-ec5cbef24bcb","body":"[ruling 2026-09-09 · founder]

**Option 1, under a condition the founder attached.** The run-event → tracker-state table survives and is the only writer of an issue's state. The two issue-level moves the lifecycle writer makes today — In Review on PR open, back to Todo on plateau or budget — become rows of the table, written under the run's holder. No lifecycle writer writes an issue state.

**The founder's condition, verbatim in substance:** keep it only if it is carried in configuration, implemented SOLID/DRY/hexagonally, and genuinely ports to any tracker — "otherwise this will go stale the minute we implement it". That condition is part of the ruling, not commentary on it. Concretely:
- the table is declared in `OperationConfig` beside `workflow_states`, never as a literal in a chain, a service or an adapter;
- its values name a `LifecycleStage` or one of the two effects, and the tracker-specific state string is resolved through `workflow_states` at the port boundary — no vendor state name appears in the table;
- exactly one seam applies a row, and it sits behind the tracker port, so a second tracker adapter satisfies the same conformance suite without a new table;
- the vocabulary → row set-equality check runs at boot, as already built.

**Why option 1 and not option 2, stated so it can be challenged.** The founder's answer was conditional ("if you keep it…"), and the condition decides the fork: a second writer that sets In Review and Todo on its own necessarily names those states where it stands, which is the staleness the condition forbids. One table, one seam, one place a new tracker has to satisfy. The ORGANIZE pass draws that inference and records it here rather than re-asking.

**Provenance, recorded because it was asked.** No outside review required this table. It was added on 2026-08-07 by an automated grooming pass on this project, justified by two measured defects in the earlier manual run: an issue held In Progress for eighteen days with no implementation, and a landing step writing issues In Progress while their PRs were open. A later pass relocated it and coined the "A8.1" shorthand. It is self-commissioned machinery answering a real defect, and the founder has now adopted it under the condition above.

**Consequences.** KOD-390's four refusals may name the session holder as a refused state writer. KOD-386, KOD-385 and KOD-383's write gating proceeds behind KOD-747's live demonstration, which is unchanged by this ruling. KOD-403's set-equality comparison has a subject. KOD-401, KOD-415, KOD-440, KOD-517 and KOD-530 are unblocked.

**What this does not settle.** Whether a stall exit writes In Review (KOD-783), which composed component holds a write lease and where its duration comes from (KOD-788), and whether the applying seam is one or two functions (KOD-443, held on KOD-755).","attachments":[],"createdAt":"2026-09-09T06:05:47.197Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-09T06:05:47.037Z","parentId":null,"resolvedAt":null,"quotedText":null,"author":{"id":"998f68f9-58c0-4c31-bdc8-2b9d8da803a2","name":"Goofy"},"onBehalfOf":null},{"id":"92487fdc-ba29-4f3d-a2d8-cb1fda0b1f36","body":"[organize 2026-09-08] founder-owned; left open — which holder identity makes the issue-level In Review / put-back moves and whether the run-event → tracker-state table survives is the founder's call; KOD-390 keeps refusing only the ruled part and the write-gating criteria stay behind KOD-747.","attachments":[],"createdAt":"2026-09-08T17:30:53.704Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-08T17:30:53.647Z","parentId":null,"resolvedAt":null,"quotedText":null,"author":{"id":"998f68f9-58c0-4c31-bdc8-2b9d8da803a2","name":"Goofy"},"onBehalfOf":null}],"hasNextPage":false}

